{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 31, :comments ["<b>And the flax and the barley were struck.</b> It means 'was broken' — like ״פַּרְעֹה נְכֹה״ (II Kings 23), ״נְכָאִים״ (Isaiah 16), and so too לֹא נֻכּוּ. It is not sound to interpret it as a term of striking — that one would explain נֻכְּתָה like הֻכְּתָה, נֻכּוּ like הֻכּוּ, for a nun does not stand in place of a heh. Rather the nun is a root in the word, and behold it is derived from ״וְשֻׁפּוּ עַצְמוֹתָיו״ (Job 33)." "<b>For the barley was in the ear.</b> It had already ripened and stood in its stalk, and they were broken and fell. And so the flax had already grown and was firm enough to stand on its stalks." "<b>The barley was in the ear.</b> It stood in its ear — like ״בְּאִבֵּי הַנָּחַל״ (Song 6)."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:51:18.628577335Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}